      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1358,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The non-past (or dictionary) form is the base form of a verb, used to express habitual or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). To express the negative, use the negative form. For most える/いる-Verbs, change the final る to ない (e.g., 食べない = 'I do not eat' or 'I will not eat'). Both the non-past and negative forms are plain forms used in casual conversation or other grammar patterns.",
