      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1355,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "In Japanese, the dictionary form and the non-past form of verbs are identical. For example, 買う is both the dictionary form and the non-past form of the verb 'to buy.' Unlike in English, this form can be used as is to express future actions ('I will buy'), habitual actions ('I regularly buy'), or general truths ('People buy a lot around Christmas').",
